00:00:06.219 --> 00:00:08.440 align:start position:0%
Europe's farming industry is set to get  a big boost after the European Union's  and main institutions reached agreement  on long-awaited reforms to the Common  Agricultural Policy or camp after  marathon talks in Luxembourg in Brussels  under the stewardship of the Irish  presidency the institution's seal the  deal on reform of the four main pillars  of the cafe which makes up the biggest  slice at the EU budget this is the first  time that there's been a co decision  function for the Parliament it's the  first time that we've had 27 soon-to-be  28 member states involved so there has  been a really challenging job  politically to do to find a way of  finding compromises finding a way  forward how we distribute money amongst  farmers very political and very  controversial I think we've come to an  acceptable compromise now that makes  sense that give countries that have  different structures and different  priorities different options while at  the same time keeping a common policy  intact the reforms were agreed after  some 18 months of negotiations capped by  four days of intensive talks they're  aimed at guaranteeing good living  standards for farmers and providing  predictable food supplies at fair prices  to consumers the package will regulate  the way farm subsidies are paid improve  the efficiency of the market in  agricultural products help foster rural  development and set out rules for cap  management and control it's been waited  to discriminate positively in favour of  young farmers and will result in a  greening of the policy by encouraging  sustainable development the task of  implementing these wide-reaching reforms  now falls to the incoming lithuanian  presidency
 

